\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Square faces on a finite graph}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A \newvocab{finite cubical 2-complex}, in the square-cell convention
used here, consists of a \vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}
#{(V,E,s,h)}, a finite set #{\mathcal P} of squares, and a specified
four-edge attaching walk for each square. Choose one orientation for
each edge and one boundary orientation and starting vertex for each
square. Its walk is
##{\partial p=(e_{p,1}^{\epsilon_{p,1}},\ldots,
 e_{p,4}^{\epsilon_{p,4}}),\qquad \epsilon_{p,j}\in\{1,-1\},}
where an exponent #{-1} means traversal against the chosen edge
orientation. Consecutive endpoints match, including the last to the
first.}

\p{Starting from the \vocabk{one-dimensional realization}{fdip-004M}, attach
one copy of #{[0,1]^2} for each #{p}, mapping its 4 sides to the
specified traversed edges, and use the quotient topology. Boundary
vertices or sides may be identified in this presentation, as in
periodic quotients; no regularity or nonpositive-curvature condition is
imposed. No higher-dimensional cubes are supplied. The squares and
their attaching maps are additional data, just as the filled face is
additional data in the [triangle comparison](fdip-004N).}

\p{The main example is the \newvocab{square torus} with periods
#{N_1,N_2\ge3}. Its vertices are
##{V=(\mathbb Z/N_1\mathbb Z)\times(\mathbb Z/N_2\mathbb Z).}
There are horizontal edges #{a_{x,y}:(x,y)\to(x+1,y)} and vertical
edges #{b_{x,y}:(x,y)\to(x,y+1)}, with indices read modulo the
periods. The square at #{(x,y)} has boundary walk
##{a_{x,y}\,b_{x+1,y}\,a_{x,y+1}^{-1}\,b_{x,y}^{-1}.}
This written order is the order of traversal. Put
##{n=|V|=N_1N_2,\qquad |E|=2n,\qquad P=|\mathcal P|=n.}
The realization is the usual square grid on a torus. The periods ensure
that each square has 4 distinct vertices and sides, although
periodic identifications occur globally.}
